of Internet) into Text mode.
I elected to do a Server install, and thus got the automatic partitioning.
The installation placed itself across both of my disks, setting up 7
partitions in all, 2 on the small drive and 5 on the large drive.

I was able to go back and do a second installation this time with the GUI,
for a workstation, this having previously failed by leaving the former
server paritions in place.

Thus, I conclude that one giant obsticle to success is disk paritioning, of
which I have yet to find a suitable practicle example.

I have gone back to try the same thing with the 7.1 distribution, but have
had no success, neither GUI nor text mode neither workstation, custom or
server.

Well, the workstation install does not proerly find the TGA display and so
it doesn't work properly.

I have gone back and installed the RedHat V6.2 Server via text mode, Gui
failed, and added the RPM's for XFree86_TGA. I have tried somethings for
configuring this, SuperProbe finds nothing, Xf86config doesn't have a choice
for TGA.

Probably my biggest complaint to date would be that on the Internet, there
are thousands of everyones copy of how to install/configure/make work a
piece or whole of Linux, in all the various distributions. Much of what
exists has been written with the understnading that the reader is standing
in familiar territory. This in my opinion is wrong. I am a seasoned Systems
Administrator, having worked with OpenVMS, Tru64 UNIX, ULTRIX, Apple,
Windows and NT and ATLAS and CP/M and I have been struggling with this LINUX
stuff folks.

My conclusion, this stuff, Linux, is not yet ready for prime time.


