No I suspect he in fatc was indicating that the BIOS could not find the
module, and that would be the case.


>
> I had the same experience: is seemed to me that the SRM did not correctly
> initialize the EISA configuration of cards which it did not need to boot.

The SRM does not initialize any cards. The small 8086 emulator in the POST
of the SROM initializes cards. By the way, whether the machine has EISA
options or not, you must always run the ECU for your target OS, in order to
get the hardware interupt controllers properly set for later booting and OS
running. If this is not doen, then al bets are off. As an example, I had a
4100 that th efloppy disk drive worked on in ALphaBIOS and in SRM. Tru64
UNIX could never find the floppy and so it was never configured into the
system at install time. The problem, no one had ever run the ECU, so the
unit was not set up properly.

>
> I would expect that you enter the card settings in the ECU, and the
BIOS/SRM
> initialize the cards so that the IO-Addresses and interrupts are set up
> accordingly. However, this does not seem to work.

You must use the proper ECU/*.cfg files.

>
> After initializing the EISA config registers manually, I was able to load
> and use the module for a DPT EISA SCSI controller.
>
> After that, I looked for information on how I could access the ECU
> information in NVRAM, and found none - the project to put the EISA
> initialization into aboot stopped at that point ...

Thats right, it doesn't work that way. You need a emulator for 8086 code.
