In article <A8vi7.13940$w75.6363766@news3.rdc2.on.home.com>,
Richard Tomkins <tomkinsr@home.com> wrote:
>
>The SRM does not initialize any cards. The small 8086 emulator in the POST
>of the SROM initializes cards. By the way, whether the machine has EISA

There is no 8086 code to run on these cards. All I expect is that the EISA
configuration space registers on the cards are set up correctly.

>You must use the proper ECU/*.cfg files.

I did (at least I hope so).

>Thats right, it doesn't work that way. You need a emulator for 8086 code.

What for?

The DPT card does not have a boot ROM, so there is no 8086 code to emulate.

The ECU-cfg-file for the card contains a list of choices for different
settings, and a description of what value to write into what register to
make that setting active.

As far as I understand it, the ECU parses these data and puts a compiled
version of it (liet of registers and values) somewhere in NVRAM.

On bootup, someone in the ROM takes these settings and writes them to the
card's config space - however, it seems this only happens with cards that
the SRM knows about, ie. the adaptec 1742 and probably some ethernet cards.

