>
>I see that DPT made some RAID SCSI adapters, powered by Intel i960
>processors, not unlike the Special Mylex units used on Alphas.

If I remember correct, my card had some Motorola (68020 or 68030) in it.

>I see that many of the SCSI cards that they made were BIOS configurable.
>Like an Adaptec, press CTRL-A during POST and get a special menu. Or that
>other manufacturer, when you pressed ALT-Q and got a special menu.

That one definitely had no ROM-based setup.

>Now, those special menus are presented to you by the processor running 16
>bit 8086 code.
>
>The cards themselves get initialized by 16 bit 8086 code. Most NIC cards do
>not have a BIOS, but those NIC cards that do a remote boot/activiation in
>fatc have a special EPROM you install on them which in fact is extended BIOS
>code.

On NIC cards, the boot rom is optional - the work just fine without boot
rom, provided you do not want to boot from the network.

Regarding the EISA setup: the card's ROM code can't set up these basic
registers, since it does not have access to the information which the ECU
placed somewhere, in some board-specific format.

So someone (ie. the mainboard BIOS) must read these data, program the EISA
card registers accorgingly, so that the 8086 ROM code can find the card
(after all, you can *move* these ROM areas in the ECU setup, so for a
confict-free ressource allocation, you need some instance that runs before
the card's BIOS!).

