"Jan Gemeiner" <Jan.Gemeiner@dresdner-bank.com> wrote in message
news:3B666F9C.805BF4D7@dresdner-bank.com...
> Now I got it.
>
> My problem was that I didn't know the BSD-style of partitioning.
>
> Here the few steps:
>
> At first, boot from your cdrom with:
>
> >>> boot dka600 -fi /kernels/generic.gz -fl root=/dev/scd0
>
> Your installation should boot now.
>
> Then you will come to the point where you have to fdisk your  harddisk.
> Now delete all "normal" partitions. Then type "b" for BSD-disklabel stuff.
> There you have to create a huge partition (over the whole harddisk), I
placed it
> at the third place. Now you create your root and your swap-partition
(first and
> second place), overlapping the huge partition (it has something of logical
> partitions).

So far, so good. Actually, with DEC's interpretation of BSD disklables (is
it like that on other OSes?), partitions "a" and "c" are used at offset 0.
Usually, "c" is the entire disk, while "a" is for root file system AND
access to partition table. In Linux, the only thing that they insist on is
that there is a partition "c", spanning the whole disk.

There were advice to let partition "a" be small, be set to "unused" and
occupy the first 2 cylinders - for reserving space for aboot. This is false
in 7.x, or at least for JumpStart CD. It will complain on overlapping
partitions (with "c") and fail to apply bootstrap. The way to go is to keep
all partitions out of that space.

> You have to set the partition type, the list is a bit different to the
normal
> partition type list.
> For the huge Partition you have to choose BSD4.2.

??? Why? On JumpStart CD, I just set it to "unused".

> When you are ready, you go back, back, back and choose diskdruid to set
the
> mount point /root for the root partition.

Most howto-s explicitely warn against using diskdruid. I was expecting
"fdisk" to be able. I guess it was valid for the RH-6.x series.

> Now you can install quite normal, after installation you reboot from cdrom
with
